                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_quintile:      `<strong>That's the right lens.</strong> Decomposing the access index by quintile shows the leak is concentrated in the poorest two quintiles — exactly where impact-per-quetzal is highest. Now you know who you're really designing for, not the comfortable average patient.`,
                    coach_r1_average:       `<strong>The average patient is hiding the problem.</strong> An overall index of 0.52 looks survivable until you split it by quintile and see the poorest dropping off a cliff. The mission isn't 'serve a healthy average' — it's reach the people who skip visits over cost.`,
                    coach_r1_payers:        `<strong>Optimising for the cash-paying 72% is how a social enterprise quietly becomes a clinic for the not-quite-poor.</strong> The patients who pay easily are not the ones the access index — or the investor's gate — is about. You'd pass on revenue and fail on mission.`,
                    coach_r1_volume:        `<strong>Total visit volume is a vanity metric here.</strong> Forty-eight thousand visits of the wrong patients doesn't move the access index for the poorest quintile. Impact-per-quetzal, not raw count, is what the gate measures.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You decomposed by quintile AND named affordability as the binding constraint. That's the lens that makes every later decision honest: price is an access decision, and you now know exactly whose access you're moving.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>You're about to design an offer on a hunch.</strong> Without quantifying the affordability map you can't say which two segments have the highest impact-per-quetzal — so you'll spend the tranche where it feels good, not where it reaches the poorest.`,
                    coach_r2_tiered:        `<strong>A tiered offer is the base-of-pyramid move.</strong> A low-cost essential tier for the poorest, cross-subsidised by paid add-ons, holds access and margin together instead of trading one for the other. Now make sure it can actually reach remote patients.`,
                    coach_r2_core:          `<strong>Core essentials protect margin — but watch the exclusions.</strong> Every condition you defer is a poorest-quintile patient who travels for nothing and doesn't come back. Lean and affordable is good; lean and unreachable is just cheap.`,
                    coach_r2_broad:         `<strong>A broad everything-bundle reaches people you can't afford to serve.</strong> Maximising depth without a cost build-up is how a 6% margin becomes a deficit before the tranche even lands. Inclusion has a price — pick the inclusions that move impact-per-quetzal.`,
                    coach_r2_noavail:       `<strong>Your offer is excellent — for someone who can reach a clinic.</strong> With almost nothing delivered through community promoters, half your target lives hours away and never arrives. Affordability without availability still equals zero patients reached. How does the offer get to the poorest where they are?`,
                    coach_r2_pharmprofit:   `<strong>Marking up the pharmacy to subsidise consultations taxes the poorest twice.</strong> Out-of-pocket medicine cost is itself an access barrier — squeezing margin from generics undoes the affordability you're trying to build. A thin-margin integrated pharmacy funds reach without pricing out the sick.`,
                    coach_r3_accredit:      `<strong>Accreditation is the durable trust signal.</strong> It costs Q250K and time, but in a market where patients can't judge clinical quality, a verifiable third-party signal is what wins back the referral NGOs and gives the rating analyst something external to point to. The competitor just advertised theirs — match the signal.`,
                    coach_r3_code:          `<strong>A self-regulation code is better than nothing — but it's a thin signal.</strong> Q40K buys speed and a gesture, not the verifiable trust that accreditation gives. Against a competitor advertising real accreditation, a voluntary code may not reassure the NGOs or the rating analyst.`,
                    coach_r3_nocert:        `<strong>You saved Q250K and bought a trust crisis.</strong> The competing chain advertises accreditation, two referral NGOs are reconsidering, and your investor's rating analyst has nothing external to verify quality with. In a market where patients can't judge clinical quality, the cost of not being trusted is the patients you never see.`,
                    coach_r3_priceout:      `<strong>You added Q5 to the fee and your margin jumps ~Q0.9M — clean.</strong> But your survey said 28% of the poorest quintile already skip visits over cost, and you set almost no cross-subsidy to catch them. Your access index just fell hard. The investor's whole gate is access-plus-viability. Who exactly did you just price out — and was that your mission?`,
                    coach_r3_hold:          `<strong>Holding the fee is safe but it doesn't fix the gap.</strong> No one is priced out, but you've also banked no margin and made no move toward break-even. Safe isn't the same as a credible plan — the gate wants a path, not a pause.`,
                    coach_r3_underprice:    `<strong>You cut fees and reached more patients — beautiful.</strong> Now show me break-even in 18 months. With little cross-subsidy behind the cut you're burning the tranche, and Q4.0M doesn't last long against a deficit. Charity that runs out of money helps no one in year three. Where's the self-sustaining engine?`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>Honest tradeoff accounting is what survives the board.</strong> Showing where access and margin diverge on the demand curve and naming which KPI you privileged is far stronger than a scorecard where everything rises. I trust the number I can reconcile.`,
                    coach_r4_glowing:       `<strong>Your scorecard shows rising margin, rising access AND a top ESG score.</strong> Reconcile those for me line by line, because the demand curve I gave you says at least two of them move in opposite directions. A glowing scorecard that doesn't add up is the fastest way to lose the room.`,
                    coach_r4_minimal:       `<strong>Headline numbers with the tradeoffs glossed over reads as a plan you can't defend.</strong> The board's whole question is which KPI you chose and why — a short pitch that dodges it tells me you didn't decide, the spreadsheet did.`,
                    coach_r4_flatsub:       `<strong>A flat subsidy that 'we'll fund later' is the subsidy trap in slow motion.</strong> The gate explicitly forbids permanent dependence. Show the subsidy <em>declining</em> as cross-subsidy and membership volume grow, or this is just a charity with a longer fuse.`,
                    coach_r4_risingsub:     `<strong>Leaning harder into subsidy to maximise patients now buys reach you can't sustain.</strong> Rising subsidy against a Q4.0M tranche is a countdown to closing clinics. Maximising year-one access at the cost of year-three survival is exactly the error the gate is built to catch.`,
                    coach_r4_paperwork:     `<strong>You're treating your cheapest source of growth capital as an afterthought.</strong> That rating is the key that unlocks up to Q6M of blended co-financing on top of the tranche. What if the rating had driven your design instead of trailing it?`,
                    coach_r4_gatewon:       `<strong>That clears the gate.</strong> An honest scorecard, a declining-subsidy glide path to break-even, and an ESG rating you designed to earn — together they unlock the blended finance and prove access and viability can hold together. That's the case the investor came for.`,

